3c942-9b54c | 2026-06-29T11:09:48Z | session:3c942260-311d-4e00-8bcc-204045ea87b3 |  \n **#2389 design pass:** done and grounded in the real code — the clean finding is that UniFFI *eliminates* 3 of the 4 byte-lane "exception" symbols outright (`nmp_app_free`, `nmp_mirror_free_bytes`, and the dispatch JSON-string/`nmp_free_string`) via object + `Vec<u8>` + typed `DispatchOutcome` lifetimes; only the update-sink hot push lane is what #2388 measures. **Codex is reviewing that design now** (your authorized milestone consult). \n  \n **#2388 benchmark:** still in flight. \n  \n I'll report back when the codex feedback and #2388 land, then finalize the #2389 scaffold design and fan out implementation. \n  \n User: [Agent task result: Dynamic workflow "Design+build+review the UniFFI-vs-C-callback byte-push benchmark (#2388)" completed] \n {"design":{"comparison_approach":"In-process Rust A/B microbench replaying ONE deterministic sequence of frame-byte buffers through two transports, so the only variable is marshalling. Lane A (C lane): the real wiring from app_lifecycle_ffi.rs — invoke an `extern \"C\" fn(*mut c_void, *const u8, usize)` through the same `Arc<dyn Fn(&[u8])>` closure shape (UpdateListener), zero-copy ptr/len, PLUS one representative \"shell consume\" memcpy out of the transient slice (mandatory for fairness: the real contract says the slice is valid only for the callback duration, so every real host copies once). Lane B (UniFFI lane): link the REAL `uniffi` runtime crate and exercise its actual lowering path for `Vec<u8>` — `RustBuffer::from_vec` (real alloc + memcpy) + `FfiConverter::<Vec<u8>>::lower` + an indirect vtable call into a Rust-implemented foreign-trait callback stub that performs the lower-bound foreign consume (memcpy RustBuffer contents into an owned Vec, then free), mirroring what Swift/Kotlin do when building a Data/ByteArray. Same buffers, same order, same allocator for both lanes. Subtract a measured empty-harness baseline (timer + index, no transport) from each. Verdict computed against a pre-registered rule.","whats_synthetic":"REAL (linked, executed, measured): the C-lane indirect call + ptr/len + shell copy; the UniFFI Rust-side cost — RustBuffer allocation, the FfiConverter lowering memcpy, and the indirect vtable callback dispatch — because we link the genuine `uniffi` crate rather than hand-simulating it. SYNTHETIC, explicitly labeled in the report as a LOWER BOUND: (1) the managed-runtime copy from RustBuffer into a real Swift `Data` / Kotlin `ByteArray` plus ARC/GC bookkeeping — modeled as a single plain memcpy+free, which is the floor of the true cost; (2) the JNI boundary surcharge (GetByteArrayElements/SetByteArrayRegion, local-ref table) — NOT executed; applied only as a pre-registered conservative multiplier band from published JNI numbers; (3) real cross-language thread/dispatch-queue handoff and on-device backpressure drop/reorder — not modeled (in-process drop/reorder is 0 by construction and reported as such, not as a real-transport claim). This is honest because the decision only needs to know whether marshalling is a small O(frame-bytes) constant or something pathological; the synthetic pieces can only make UniFFI look CHEAPER than reality, and the decision rule forbids a collapse verdict on synthetic evidence unless the surcharged estimate is comfortably under budget (otherwise it escalates to a real foreign-side measurement).","workload_model":"legacy decision 0055 diff-shaped, omit-unchanged regime. Three size buckets, deterministic fixed buffers (random transport is fine since transport carries opaque bytes; sizes chosen from the real encoder envelope + visible-window=80): SMALL incremental diff 256 B–2 KB (1–3 changed typed-projection sidecar entries), MEDIUM batch/scroll diff 2–8 KB, LARGE full snapshot 16–64 KB (account switch / epoch bump of the <=80-item visible window). Weighted mix 80% small / 15% medium / 5% large (omit-unchanged makes small dominant). Cadence ground