019f0-05ac8 | 2026-06-29T12:11:00Z | session:019f0dc3-5b56-79d1-a14b-5746c93e5879 | ## Zero-tolerance on hacks, debt, and fragmentation \n  \n **No temporary hacks. Ever.** This is a strict, non-negotiable rule enforced pedantically: \n  \n - No "for now" workarounds, stubs that stay, or `// TODO: fix this properly` comments left \n   in production code. A staged fix is allowed *only* when a GitHub issue labeled \n   `status:staged` documents every stage with a completion deadline. An unplanned, undocumented "temporary" \n   measure is categorically forbidden — there is no such thing as acceptable technical debt. [...] User: we should only have CI checks that help idnetify issues as we build, but we shouldn't keep unnecessary CI gates that will just slow us down while things are supposed to be broken (i.e. mid refactor) \n  [...] User: On the pursuit of this epic we can take any shortcuts we want to expedite things, for example we can just delete or disable wholesale test suite that no longer applies, remove CI, move the nmp-gallery apps out of this repo into its own repo like we did with chirp and update it after the fact (we should probably do this anyway and keep it as a separate repo); anything goes
019f0-22da8 | 2026-06-29T12:11:11Z | session:019f0dc3-5b56-79d1-a14b-5746c93e5879 |  \n Construction is composable. A feature can build a draft like: \n  \n ```text \n reply_to(event) \n react_to(event, "+") \n article(title, body) \n nip29_group_reply(group_id, event) \n podcast_episode_publish(show_id, episode_id) \n ``` \n  \n Then finalizers may add protocol-specific envelope details: \n  [...] 5. Make `register_defaults` stop being the conceptual app path. There should be mandatory substrate plus explicit feature bundles. Social, DMs, zaps, longform are feature choices, not “defaults.” \n  [...]   .feature(nip02_follow_list()) \n   .feature(nip51_lists()) \n   .feature(nip29_groups()) \n   .feature(search()) \n   .feature(home_feed()) \n   .feature(my_app_feature()) \n   .start() \n ``` \n  \n A `Feature` is installed capability: actions, parsers, projections, query types, capability needs. It is not an open screen. `nmp-defaults` should become a library of named feature bundles, not a magic black box. [...] A `Feature` should be the main composition unit. It bundles: \n  \n - typed views/read models \n - typed commands/writes \n - subscription demand \n - ingest parsers \n - capability needs \n - projection encoders [...] **What Developers Need To Know** \n They should know: \n  \n - Which feature bundles their app installs. \n - Which `LiveQuery` a screen opens. \n - Which typed projection the screen renders. \n - Which typed commands publish events. \n - When to close query handles. \n - When a query is default outbox-routed vs explicitly relay-pinned. [...] They should not need to know: \n  \n - `SnapshotRegistry` \n - projection tiers \n - muted observers \n - replay shapes \n - relay fanout details \n - NIP-65 mailbox routing internals \n - `open_interest` plumbing \n - FlatBuffer sidecar mechanics \n - cache/store replay mechanics \n  \n That is the simplification: **keep NMP’s internal correctness machinery, but make the app model small and explicit.** [...] The `Kernel` has become a god object. It owns store, relay state, projection registries, action ledger, publish engine, observers, auth, accounts, provenance, cache serves, pull cursors, lifecycle, and multiple side registries. Some state belongs there, but not as one enormous mental object. See [kernel/mod.rs](/Users/pablofernandez/Work/nostr-multi-platform/crates/nmp-core/src/kernel/mod.rs:227). \n  \n Projection delivery is directionally right, but too layered. Keep FlatBuffers. Do not keep the current level of registry/tick-observer/feed-author-provider/declaration/incremental/rev/manifest/sidecar choreography as an app-visible model. `SnapshotRegistry` is doing too many jobs at once: [snapshot_registry.rs](/Users/pablofernandez/Work/nostr-multi-platform/crates/nmp-core/src/kernel/snapshot_registry.rs:114). The native embed sidecar feedback loop is especially suspect because it decodes a just-emitted frame to create another projection for a later tick. [...] 4. Split `SnapshotRegistry` into real owners: projection registry, projection delivery contract, tick observers, feed-author helpers. Same facade, simpler internals. [...] Internal complexity is justified only if it protects a real invariant: replay-before-live, privacy, route provenance, bounded FFI, no polling, signer safety, teardown, or cross-platform consistency. \n
019f0-9675d | 2026-06-29T12:11:20Z | session:019f0dc3-5b56-79d1-a14b-5746c93e5879 | ## What belongs in NMP crates vs. app-specific Rust crates \n  \n **NMP crates (`crates/`)** provide reusable Nostr infrastructure. A feature belongs in an NMP crate when it is a general building block that any Nostr application — or a meaningful subset of Nostr applications — could use directly. Examples: relay management, signing, NIP implementations, event storage, timeline projection, encrypted messaging, identity. The test: *"would this crate be useful to a completely different Nostr app?"* If yes, it is an NMP crate. \n  \n **App Rust crates (`apps/<app>/`)** hold the Rust side of features that are specific to that application's domain and would not generalize to other Nostr apps. Example: a podcast app's audio playback engine, chapter parsing, or feed-subscription state. These belong in the app's own Rust crates, not in NMP. NMP does not accumulate app-specific logic. \n  \n The line is not protocol vs. product — a product-level feature (e.g., NIP-29 group chat, Marmot MLS encrypted groups) belongs in an NMP crate if other Nostr apps would use it. The line is **generic Nostr building block vs. this app's proprietary domain**. [...] **App Rust crates (`apps/<app>/`)** hold the Rust side of features that are specific to that application's domain and would not generalize to other Nostr apps. Example: a podcast app's audio playback engine, chapter parsing, or feed-subscription state. These belong in the app's own Rust crates, not in NMP. NMP does not accumulate app-specific logic. \n  \n The line is not protocol vs. product — a product-level feature (e.g., NIP-29 group chat, Marmot MLS encrypted groups) belongs in an NMP crate if other Nostr apps would use it. The line is **generic Nostr building block vs. this app's proprietary domain**. [...] The line is not protocol vs. product — a product-level feature (e.g., NIP-29 group chat, Marmot MLS encrypted groups) belongs in an NMP crate if other Nostr apps would use it. The line is **generic Nostr building block vs. this app's proprietary domain**. [...] - protocol-owned reusable projections like follow-list living in `nmp-nip02`, not app FFI crates
019f0-6a609 | 2026-06-29T12:11:20Z | session:019f0dc3-5b56-79d1-a14b-5746c93e5879 | A request from one app is evidence, not permission to specialize the framework. \n Do not add app-named helpers, bespoke publish/read commands, hard-coded product \n defaults, operator policy, compatibility shims, or "quick" shared-crate \n workarounds just because a consuming app needs them. First ask whether the \n missing piece is a reusable Nostr mechanism. If yes, add the generic mechanism \n once. If no, implement it in the app's Rust core. If the answer is unclear, \n update the owning issue or ADR before writing code.
