91a86-3f79b | 2026-07-03T09:42:59Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | ## No — don't build the wallet money-safety journal on Trellis. (The read projection already rides it.) \n  \n **The one fact that settles it:** Trellis graphs are **in-memory, per-session, and die with the process by design** — built fresh in `FeedSessionTrellisAdapter::new`, torn down on `close_scope`, with zero persistence in `trellis-core`. The wallet journal's *entire reason to exist* is surviving process death after an irreversible external effect (a mint spend). Trellis can't even represent that problem, let alone solve it. [...] | | Trellis (ADR-0075) | Wallet operation journal | \n |---|---|---| \n | Shape | Reactive **read** dependency-graph reconciliation | Durable **write** saga with external side effects | \n | Semantics | **Convergent + declarative** — diff desired vs. actual; *close is always safe, reopen always possible* | **Monotonic + effectful** — states only advance; *a mint spend can't be "closed"*; at-most-once | \n | "Reconcile on restart" | Replay a trace, check incremental recompute == full recompute (deterministic, local) | Query the **mint** (external authority) for proof state, then publish or unlock (non-deterministic, remote) | \n | Persistence / I/O | Owns none — ADR-0075 assigns I/O, store, actor integration to **NMP** | *Is* the whole point | \n  \n The tempting overlaps — "graph transactions," "deterministic replay," "trace/oracle" — are all **false friends**: same words, opposite category. Trellis's replay is a *derivation-consistency* oracle over local inputs; wallet recovery reconciles against a remote authority whose answers aren't a function of local state. [...] **What to use instead — no new substrate needed.** There's no generic durable-saga crate in the workspace, and building one now would be a one-consumer abstraction. The design doc already specifies the right shape: `nmp-wallet` owns an **actor-owned durable journal** persisted through NMP storage, with command-shaped reducers that never await mint HTTP, capability-lane mint workers returning raw results, and the existing publish engine handling `PublishPending`. That's the standard NMP actor pattern + a persisted journal. (If a second saga ever appears — `nmp-marmot`'s pending-MLS-autopublish is the likely candidate — extract a generic saga substrate *then*, post-hoc.)
