91a86-17771 | 2026-07-03T10:27:54Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | My read: **Trellis (ADR-0075) is a reconciliation substrate for the reactive *read* side** — graph transactions, dependency identity, collection diffs, scoped teardown, output-frame lifecycle, deterministic replay — living *below typed read sessions*. The wallet operation journal is a different animal: a **durable write-side saga** (`Draft→MintPending→MintSettled→PublishPending→Settled/Unknown/Failed`) whose whole job is crash-recovery and reconciling against *external* authority (the mint) so no double-spend/double-pay survives process death. [...] - **Saga (1)** writes **pre-effect**: "about to consume proofs A,B for a mint spend." That pre-record *is* the at-most-once mechanism. Durable, money-critical. [...] - **Trail (3)** writes **post-observation**: "a deletion arrived / a probe found C spent / a nutzap redeemed." Diagnostic, bounded. [...] - **State (2)** is the fold over the post-observation facts. [...] The load-bearing correction: **you can't read 2/3 out of 1**, because the saga only knows *locally-initiated* operations — and most of what makes the wallet chaotic (inbound token arrivals, NIP-09 deletions from your *other* devices, incoming nutzaps) never touches the saga at all. So it's not a subset relationship. It's **producer/consumer**: the saga *emits facts into* the trail (`MintSettled`, `Unknown→reconciled`, `Failed`) so the trail can explain locally-caused shape changes too — but their schemas never merge (money-critical pre-effect records must not live in a diagnostic log with bounded eviction).
91a86-ced00 | 2026-07-03T10:27:54Z | session:91a86fdf-624c-446e-9b38-0fb02085121f | - **Saga (1)** writes **pre-effect**: "about to consume proofs A,B for a mint spend." That pre-record *is* the at-most-once mechanism. Durable, money-critical. [...] - **Trail (3)** writes **post-observation**: "a deletion arrived / a probe found C spent / a nutzap redeemed." Diagnostic, bounded. [...] - **State (2)** is the fold over the post-observation facts. [...] The load-bearing correction: **you can't read 2/3 out of 1**, because the saga only knows *locally-initiated* operations — and most of what makes the wallet chaotic (inbound token arrivals, NIP-09 deletions from your *other* devices, incoming nutzaps) never touches the saga at all. So it's not a subset relationship. It's **producer/consumer**: the saga *emits facts into* the trail (`MintSettled`, `Unknown→reconciled`, `Failed`) so the trail can explain locally-caused shape changes too — but their schemas never merge (money-critical pre-effect records must not live in a diagnostic log with bounded eviction). [...] I had said "in-memory, rebuild on restart." Fable caught that the naive rebuild is weak: after restart, every pre-session cause collapses to "this is just what the relays said" — you lose exactly the explanation you wanted ("balance dropped because last session a mint probe found spent proofs"). But the durable causal tier **already exists in the protocol**: NIP-60 `kind:7376` history (which the design doc already *requires* for every balance-changing op, and `nmp-nip60/src/history_event.rs` already has the codec) plus the `del` field on token events. So: \n  \n - **Don't build a durable local trail.** `kind:7376` + `del` tags *are* the durable, cross-device, encrypted causal record. \n - **The in-memory ring is the session-local high-resolution overlay** — mint-probe verdicts, arrival provenance, saga correlation ids — the stuff too fine-grained or too transient for 7376. [...] - **The in-memory ring is the session-local high-resolution overlay** — mint-probe verdicts, arrival provenance, saga correlation ids — the stuff too fine-grained or too transient for 7376. \n - **Restart** = fold 7376 into coarse pre-session facts, then the ring accumulates fine facts from there. Two resolutions, one timeline. [...] - **Restart** = fold 7376 into coarse pre-session facts, then the ring accumulates fine facts from there. Two resolutions, one timeline. [...] // The fact stream — post-observation, each carrying WHY + provenance. \n enum WalletFact { \n     TokenAdded     { token_event: EventId, amount: Msat, mint: MintUrl, via: Provenance }, \n     TokenDeleted   { token_event: EventId, cause: DeleteCause },      // NIP-09 in, or local rollover \n     MintProbed     { proof: ProofRef, verdict: ProofVerdict },        // Spent / Unspent / Unknown \n     NutzapRedeemed { nutzap: EventId, amount: Msat, sender: PubkeyRef }, \n     SagaTransition { op: CorrelationId, from: OpState, to: OpState },  // producer wiring from concern 1 \n     StateRebuilt   { from: Vec<EventId> },                            // genesis fact after restart \n } [...] - **A per-atom last-cause index** (`token_event_id → last WalletFact that touched it`) → answers *"why is **this specific** proof/token here?"* This is O(current state), not O(traffic), so a nutzap flood can't evict the cause of a token you still hold. Fable's addition — and it's the half of your question the ring alone can't answer. [...] 1. **The reducer must be confluent** — terminal state order-*insensitive* (a delete arriving before its token must tombstone, not no-op), even though the trail is order-*sensitive*. Otherwise two devices show different balances and the trail "explains" a state that shouldn't exist. \n 2. **The ring is never a rebuild authority** — restart rebuilds from Nostr + saga reconcile, entering as a `StateRebuilt` genesis fact. (Name it explicitly so nobody later "optimizes" restart into ring-replay.) [...] 4. **Privacy at the type level** — fact payloads carry event ids, op ids, amounts-by-unit, and canonical mint URLs only when already public via `kind:10019`; never proofs or secrets. Bake it into the types, not a display-edge filter. [...] Net design in one line: **`nmp-wallet` owns an event-sourced, confluent reducer; state is the fold, the causal trail is a bounded in-memory ring + a per-atom cause index over the same facts, the saga feeds facts in as a producer, and `kind:7376` is the durable tier — all separate from the money-safety journal's persisted at-most-once records.** [...] **3. What you're actually asking for: the causal trail.** "*Why* is the state this shape — what sequence of events produced it?" A deletion removed proofs A,B; a mint probe found C already spent and reconciled it; a nutzap arrived and added 21. That's an **in-memory, annotated delta log** — rebuildable, non-money-critical, and exactly the "how did we get here" journal you want. [...] - File split: saga.rs / fact.rs / trail.rs / ledger.rs (+ledger_state.rs, split later purely for the 500-LOC hard cap). \n - `TokenAdded` carries `proofs: Vec<ProofAtom>` not a flat amount, so the reducer owns proof-set membership, not just token-event membership. \n - Saga emits an explicit `WalletSagaEvent` from `transition()` (not a callback/drain) — one-directional producer wiring into `WalletFact::from`. \n - `rebuild_from` is a distinct entry point from `apply()`, folding seeds into one `StateRebuilt` genesis fact. \n - Privacy test: exhaustive-match + sealed marker trait over field types, not a runtime-only check. [...] - Mint-probe-found-spent: `mint_probe_found_spent_excludes_only_that_proof_from_balance` (+ `mint_probe_spent_verdict_is_absorbing`, added after codex found Spent wasn't monotonic). \n - Crash-after-MintSettled / restart-reconcile: both in `tests/journal_reconcile.rs`. \n  \n ## Codex review pass caught 4 real must-fix bugs before I opened the PR (all fixed + regression-tested) \n 1. `rebuild_from` wasn't actually tombstone-confluent — it bypassed the guard `fold()` used, so a tombstone-seed-then-live-seed for the same token would resurrect it. Fixed by routing both through shared `WalletDerivedState::apply_token_live/apply_token_tombstone`. \n 2. `MintProbed` was last-writer-wins — a stale `Spent -> Unspent` probe would resurrect balance. Made `Spent` absorbing. [...] Assistant: The money-critical logic is correct: `apply_token_live` refuses to revive a tombstoned token (delete-before-add stays dead), `apply_token_tombstone` is idempotent and pre-add-safe, and `apply_mint_probe` makes `Spent` absorbing (a stale `Unspent` can't resurrect balance). Both fold and rebuild route through the same guards — confluence holds. `WalletDerivedState` is deliberately non-`Serialize` (durable tier = kind:7375/7376). I'm confident in this.
